---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { LINKS, EMAIL, REPO, USED_BY, SITE, CADKIT_VERSION, repoFile } from '../../config';

const title = 'Community';
const description =
	'How to ask questions, report bugs without sharing private drawings, contribute under the clean-room rules, and how cadkit is governed.';
---

<PageLayout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Community' }]} eyebrow="Community">
	<div class="ck-container">
		<nav class="ck-toc" aria-label="On this page">
			<ul>
				<li><a href="#questions">Questions</a></li>
				<li><a href="#issues">Bug reports</a></li>
				<li><a href="#contribute">Contribute</a></li>
				<li><a href="#conduct">Code of conduct</a></li>
				<li><a href="#governance">Governance</a></li>
				<li><a href="#support">Support</a></li>
				<li><a href="#used-by">Used by</a></li>
				<li><a href="#cite">Cite or announce</a></li>
			</ul>
		</nav>
		<Prose>
			<h2 id="questions">Questions and ideas</h2>
			<p>
				Ask in <a href={LINKS.discussions}>GitHub Discussions</a>: how to read a particular file, which API fits a
				task, or an idea for a new feature. Start with the <a href="/docs/">documentation</a> and the Rust API
				reference on <a href={LINKS.docsRs}>docs.rs</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="issues">Bug reports</h2>
			<p>
				<a href={`${REPO}/issues/new/choose`}>Open an issue</a> with the cadkit version, the interface you used
				(Rust, Python, JavaScript, C or the CLI), what you expected and what happened, and a minimal file that shows
				the problem.
			</p>
			<Callout variant="caution" title="Never attach private drawings">
				<p>
					Issues are public. Do not attach confidential or client drawings, or excerpts of them: drawings carry
					names, addresses, coordinates and layer names that you may not be allowed to share. Reduce the problem to
					a synthetic file you drew yourself or to a public sample. Reports with a reduced, shareable file are
					answered first.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<p>
				Security vulnerabilities are never reported in a public issue; see <a href="/security/">Security</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="contribute">Contribute</h2>
			<p>
				Pull requests are welcome. Read <a href={repoFile('CONTRIBUTING.md')}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a>,{' '}
				<a href={repoFile('AGENTS.md')}>AGENTS.md</a> and{' '}
				<a href={repoFile('docs/ARCHITECTURE.md')}>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</a> first; they bind human and automated
				contributors alike. In short:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Clean-room.</strong> Format knowledge comes only from allowed references (the public Open Design
					Specification for DWG, the MIT-licensed ACadSharp, ezdgn and GDAL DGN driver, public samples and your own
					analysis). Code from copyleft-licensed CAD projects, vendor SDKs and anything decompiled is off limits; if
					you have read such code, say so before contributing in that area.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Provenance.</strong> Every source you rely on gets a row in{' '}
					<a href={LINKS.provenanceSource}>docs/PROVENANCE.md</a>, and ported logic is attributed.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No panics on any input.</strong> Sizes and counts from a file are checked against the reader limits
					before allocating; recoverable problems become warnings.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tests with synthetic fixtures</strong> that document the byte layout; update the docs and the
					Unreleased section of the changelog.
				</li>
				<li>Code, comments and documentation are in English.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="conduct">Code of conduct</h2>
			<p>
				Everyone taking part follows the <a href={repoFile('CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md')}>Code of Conduct</a>, adapted from
				the Contributor Covenant 2.1. Report unacceptable behaviour to{' '}
				<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.conduct}`}>{EMAIL.conduct}</a>; reports are handled in confidence.
			</p>

			<h2 id="governance">Governance</h2>
			<p>
				cadkit currently has one maintainer, the GitHub account <a href="https://github.com/TNYCL">TNYCL</a>, who
				reviews and merges pull requests, sets the roadmap and format scope, cuts releases and handles security
				reports. Day-to-day changes are decided in pull requests; larger ones (a new format, a new binding, a change
				to the document model or a breaking API change) start as an issue or a discussion. Contributors who land
				several substantial, well-tested changes and review others' work can be invited to become maintainers. The
				full text is in <a href={repoFile('GOVERNANCE.md')}>GOVERNANCE.md</a>; contact the maintainers at{' '}
				<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.maintainers}`}>{EMAIL.maintainers}</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="support">Support</h2>
			<p>
				cadkit is maintained by volunteers and offers no service-level guarantee. For anything that is not a
				question, a bug or a vulnerability, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.hello}`}>{EMAIL.hello}</a>. See{' '}
				<a href={repoFile('SUPPORT.md')}>SUPPORT.md</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="used-by">Used by</h2>
			<ul>
				{
					USED_BY.map((u) => (
						<li>
							<a href={u.url}>{u.name}</a>, {u.description}, uses{' '}
							{u.modules.map((mod, i) => (
								<>
									{i > 0 && (i === u.modules.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}
									<code>{mod}</code>
								</>
							))}
							.
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
			<p>
				An adopter is not a sponsor: cadkit stays vendor-neutral, and product-specific rules, customer data and
				domain logic of any adopter live outside the repository. Using cadkit? Say so in{' '}
				<a href={LINKS.discussions}>Discussions</a>.
			</p>

			<h2 id="cite">Cite or announce</h2>
			<p>
				Saying that your software uses, supports or is built with cadkit is welcome without asking, as is linking to
				the project with the name or the unmodified logo (<a href="/brand/">logo files</a>,{' '}
				<a href="/trademarks/">name and logo policy</a>). To cite cadkit in writing, name the version you used:
			</p>
			<pre class="ck-cite"><code>cadkit contributors. cadkit: an open CAD engine in pure Rust. Version {CADKIT_VERSION}. {SITE}/ (source: {REPO}).</code></pre>
		</Prose>
	</div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	.ck-toc ul {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--ck-space-2) var(--ck-space-4);
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0 0 var(--ck-space-8);
		padding: var(--ck-space-3) 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--ck-border);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ck-border);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.ck-cite {
		white-space: pre-wrap;
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}
</style>
